---
import Simple from '../layouts/Simple.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site.js';
import pricing from '../data/pricing.json';
const updated = '29 September 2026';
---
<Simple schema={{ '@type': 'WebPage', name: 'Refund policy', url: 'https://prewarmed.org/refund-policy/' }}
  title="Refund Policy. Prewarmed.org"
  description="The refund terms for prewarmed inboxes and domains ordered through Warm Inboxes. Stated plainly, and worth reading before you order rather than after."
  eyebrow="Legal" h1="Refund policy">
  <p class="muted">Last updated {updated}.</p>

  <p class="note"><strong>There are no refunds.</strong> Not after delivery, not on partial use, and not on unused inboxes within a paid term. If that is a problem, do not order, which is exactly why it is on its own page rather than in the small print.</p>

  <p>{site.disclosure} Orders are placed and paid on warminboxes.com, so their terms govern your purchase. This page states the policy plainly because burying it would be both unfair and, for a business running ads, a chargeback waiting to happen.</p>

  <h2>Why it works this way</h2>
  <p>What you are buying has already been spent before you receive it. A prewarmed domain has had thirty days of aging and twenty-one days of warming put into it, fifty-one days of elapsed calendar time that cannot be returned to stock and resold. The moment a domain and its mailboxes are handed to you, that work is consumed. Mailbox licences are also paid to Google or Microsoft up front for the term.</p>
  <p>This is the ordinary arrangement for provisioned infrastructure rather than anything unusual, but it does mean the decision to buy is worth making carefully.</p>

  <h2>If a domain stops landing later</h2>
  <p>Every provider has domains that eventually stop landing. Be clear about what happens here: <strong>we do not repair burnt domains and we do not swap them.</strong> Repair takes weeks and rarely restores the reputation, so a domain that stops landing after handover is retired rather than rehabilitated.</p>
  <p>What you do instead is order a fresh batch, and fresh domains and inboxes ship the same day. That is faster than any repair and it is the better starting point for deliverability. It is a new order rather than a refund on the old one, which is why it sits on this page: plan for it by holding back a little spare capacity, and a burn costs you no sending days.</p>

  <h2>What is not covered</h2>
  <p>Deliverability outcomes are not covered by any policy, here or anywhere honest. Infrastructure decides whether your mail gets a fair hearing. Your list, your copy and your sending behaviour decide the rest, and those are yours.</p>
  <p>In particular, a domain damaged by sending to unverified data, by ignoring unsubscribes, or by sending far above the recommended volume is not a fault in what was supplied. The <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works</a> page sets out the volumes we recommend and run ourselves.</p>

  <h2>If something is wrong with what you received</h2>
  <p>If an order arrives incomplete, misconfigured, or not as described (records missing, mailboxes short, the wrong platform), that is a supply problem, not a refund question, and it gets fixed. Contact Warm Inboxes support with your order reference. If you cannot get a response, write to <a href={`mailto:${site.correctionsEmail}`}>{site.correctionsEmail}</a> and we will chase it from this side.</p>

  <h2>Chargebacks</h2>
  <p>Raising a chargeback instead of contacting support gets an account closed and does not get anything fixed faster. Talk to us first; almost everything that looks like a billing problem is a supply problem with a straightforward answer.</p>

  <h2>Delivery</h2>
  <p>{pricing.policy.delivery}. If an order has not arrived within that window, it is a support question rather than a refund question, and the answer is usually quick.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p><a href={`mailto:${site.correctionsEmail}`}>{site.correctionsEmail}</a>, or Warm Inboxes support for anything attached to an order. prewarmed.org and Warm Inboxes are operated by {site.entity}.</p>
</Simple>
